In this lesson we'll look at two ways to summarise a list of numbers: the mean and the standard deviation. The mean is just the average, the balance point of the data. You add everything up and divide by how many values you have. That gives you one number that sits right in the middle. Now, the standard deviation tells you how spread out the numbers are around that mean. Here's the idea. First, find how far each value is from the mean. Some are above, some are below, so we square those distances to get rid of the negative signs. Then we average those squared distances, and finally take the square root. That final number is the standard deviation. What does it mean? If it's small, your data is clustered tightly around the mean. If it's large, the data is scattered far and wide. One quick note: some textbooks use a slightly different formula, dividing by one less than the sample size instead of the full count. Both versions appear in Year 10A work, so keep an eye out. Now let's see it in action.